  "explanation": "<div class=\"be-intro\">\n  <h2>The present simple of <em>be</em>: Form</h2>\n  <p>Welcome! The verb <strong>be</strong> (am / is / are) is one of the first verbs you need in English. You use it almost every day — to say who you are, how you feel, where something is, and much more.</p>\n  <p>In English, <strong>be</strong> has <strong>three</strong> present forms. Learn them well — they appear in almost every conversation.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"be-note\"><strong>Remember:</strong> I → <em>am</em> &nbsp;·&nbsp; he / she / it → <em>is</em> &nbsp;·&nbsp; we / you / they → <em>are</em></div>\n\n<figure class=\"be-figure\">\n  <img src=\"images/grammar/a1/be-form-chart.png\" alt=\"Chart: present simple of be — positive, negative, questions, and short answers\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" />\n  <figcaption>Form chart: positive · negative · questions · short answers</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<div class=\"be-chart\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Present simple of be: positive, negative, and questions\">\n  <h3 class=\"be-chart-title\">The present simple of be: Form</h3>\n  <div class=\"be-form-grid\">\n    <div class=\"be-col be-col-pos\">\n      <span class=\"be-col-h\">Positive</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">I am / I’m</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">You are / You’re</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">He is / He’s</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">She is / She’s</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">It is / It’s</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">We are / We’re</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">They are / They’re</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-col be-col-neg\">\n      <span class=\"be-col-h\">Negative</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">I am not / I’m not</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">You are not / You’re not / You aren’t</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">He is not / He’s not / He isn’t</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">She is not / She’s not / She isn’t</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">It is not / It’s not / It isn’t</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">We are not / We’re not / We aren’t</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">They are not / They’re not / They aren’t</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-col be-col-q\">\n      <span class=\"be-col-h\">Question</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">Am I …?</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">Are you …?</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">Is he …?</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">Is she …?</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">Is it …?</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">Are we …?</span>\n      <span class=\"be-pill\">Are they …?</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"be-short\">\n    <div class=\"be-short-grid\">\n      <div>\n        <h4>Short answers — Yes</h4>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">Yes, I am</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">Yes, you are</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">Yes, he is</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">Yes, she is</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">Yes, it is</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">Yes, we are</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">Yes, they are</span>\n      </div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Short answers — No</h4>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">No, I’m not</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">No, you aren’t / you’re not</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">No, he isn’t / he’s not</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">No, she isn’t / she’s not</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">No, it isn’t / it’s not</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">No, we aren’t / we’re not</span>\n        <span class=\"be-pill\">No, they aren’t / they’re not</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-rule\">\n      <strong>Important rule:</strong> We <em>cannot</em> use short forms in <strong>positive</strong> short answers.<br>\n      <span class=\"be-bad\">A: Are you happy? B: Yes, I’m.</span><br>\n      <span class=\"be-ok\">A: Are you happy? B: Yes, I am.</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The three forms (say them aloud)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>I am</strong> (I’m)</li>\n  <li><strong>He / She / It is</strong> (he’s / she’s / it’s)</li>\n  <li><strong>We / You / They are</strong> (we’re / you’re / they’re)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>They</strong> = people <em>and</em> things (more than one).</p>\n\n<h3>He, she, it, they — who or what?</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>We use <strong>he</strong> for a man or a boy.</li>\n  <li>We use <strong>she</strong> for a woman or a girl.</li>\n  <li>We use <strong>it</strong> for a thing, an animal (sometimes), the weather, time, and “this/that”.</li>\n  <li>We use <strong>they</strong> for people <em>and</em> for things (plural).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"be-example\">He’s a little boy.</div>\n<div class=\"be-example\">She’s beautiful.</div>\n<div class=\"be-example\">I like this TV. It’s very big.</div>\n<div class=\"be-example\">I love Sara and Jonas. <strong>They are</strong> my friends.</div>\n<div class=\"be-example\">I love these chairs. <strong>They are</strong> very beautiful.</div>\n\n<h3>Don’t forget the subject</h3>\n<p>In English we almost always need a <strong>subject</strong> (I, you, she, the weather, Tom…) <em>before</em> the verb. Beginners often forget this — please don’t!</p>\n<div class=\"be-example\"><span class=\"be-ok\">It is cold.</span> &nbsp; <span class=\"be-bad\">Is cold.</span></div>\n<div class=\"be-example\"><span class=\"be-ok\">Sally is a wonderful woman.</span> &nbsp; <span class=\"be-bad\">Is a wonderful woman.</span></div>\n\n<h3>Contracted (short) forms</h3>\n<p>We often use short forms with personal pronouns: <strong>’m</strong>, <strong>’s</strong>, <strong>’re</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>I’m sad.</li>\n  <li>You’re tired.</li>\n  <li>She’s from Scotland.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We can also use <strong>’s</strong> with names and places:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Laura’s beautiful.</li>\n  <li>London’s an expensive city.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In everyday American English, short forms are very common when we speak and in messages to friends.</p>\n\n<h3>Contractions in short answers</h3>\n<p>We can use contractions in <strong>negative</strong> short answers. We do <strong>not</strong> use them in <strong>positive</strong> short answers.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><span class=\"be-ok\">Yes, I am.</span> &nbsp; <span class=\"be-bad\">Yes, I’m.</span></li>\n  <li><span class=\"be-ok\">Yes, she is.</span> &nbsp; <span class=\"be-bad\">Yes, she’s.</span></li>\n  <li><span class=\"be-ok\">Yes, they are.</span> &nbsp; <span class=\"be-bad\">Yes, they’re.</span></li>\n  <li><span class=\"be-ok\">No, I’m not.</span> / <span class=\"be-ok\">No, she isn’t.</span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr style=\"margin:2rem 0;border:none;border-top:1px solid #e5e7eb\" />\n\n<h2>The present simple of be — USE</h2>\n<p>We use <strong>be</strong> to talk about many everyday things. Look at the picture chart, then read the examples slowly.</p>\n\n<figure class=\"be-figure\">\n  <img src=\"images/grammar/a1/be-use-chart.png\" alt=\"Chart: different uses of be — introducing people, age, job, nationality, feelings, colour, prices, weather, time, descriptions\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" />\n  <figcaption>Use chart: ten common situations for am / is / are</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<div class=\"be-chart\">\n  <h3 class=\"be-chart-title\">Different uses of be</h3>\n  <div class=\"be-use-grid\" style=\"padding:1rem\">\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">👋</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Introducing people</h4>\n        <p>I<em>’m</em> Steven, and this <em>is</em> Isabella. And this <em>is</em> my daughter, Alicia.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🎂</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Age</h4>\n        <p>I<em>’m</em> 24 years old, and my father <em>is</em> 50.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">💼</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Job</h4>\n        <p>I<em>’m</em> a teacher, and my wife <em>is</em> a doctor.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🏳️</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Nationality / origin</h4>\n        <p>Alex <em>is</em> from Ireland, and Sonia <em>is</em> from Spain.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">💭</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Feelings</h4>\n        <p>I<em>’m</em> scared. She <em>is</em> very tired.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🎨</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Colour</h4>\n        <p>Our dog <em>is</em> black. His eyes <em>are</em> blue.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">💲</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Prices</h4>\n        <p>It<em>’s</em> seven dollars. This T-shirt <em>is</em> twenty dollars.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">☀️</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Weather</h4>\n        <p>It<em>’s</em> sunny today. It <em>is</em> very cold this morning.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⏰</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Time</h4>\n        <p>What time <em>is</em> it? It<em>’s</em> ten past four.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"be-use-card\">\n      <div class=\"be-use-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🔍</div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Descriptions</h4>\n        <p>He <em>is</em> very short and thin. These cars <em>are</em> old and cheap.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Study tip for absolute beginners</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>First learn: <strong>I am / he is / they are</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Then practice questions: <strong>Am I…? Is she…? Are they…?</strong></li>\n  <li>Then practice short answers: <strong>Yes, I am. / No, I’m not.</strong></li>\n  <li>Finally, notice <em>when</em> we use be (age, job, weather…).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When you finish reading, go back to <strong>Exercises</strong> and try Exercise 1 again. Mistakes are normal — that is how you learn!</p>\n",
